4.94.m:
Her mother, afflicted in time by fever and burning heat, / went to the waters of a river, died, and went to heaven.
Sumantu performed the funeral rites for her. / Then Sumantu, gradually abandoning grief and sorrow, [thought]: / 'A householder without a wife cannot perform religious duties anywhere.' / Thinking thus in his mind, his heart became eager for marriage.
He married Karkaśā, the daughter of the sage Devala. / That Karkaśā was extremely ill-tempered and constantly spoke harsh words;
she was irascible, perverse, shameless, fond of quarrelling, / inept at household duties, and skilled [only] in eating;
delighted by discord, yet angry with her kin. / Meanwhile, Śīlā grew day by day in her father's house;
she played pleasant childhood games with her female friends, / adorning the interior of the house, doors, thresholds, and archways again and again / with swastikas, lotuses, and conches / using the fourfold colours: red, yellow, white, and black.
